# TypeScript & React

## TypeScript

- Prefer `unknown` over `any`; narrow before use.
- Use precise types for public APIs/props; avoid loose `object` / `Function`.
- Prefer `interface` for object shapes that may be extended; `type` for unions/intersections/mapped types.
- Don’t use non-null assertions (`!`) unless the invariant is obvious and local.
- Prefer `async`/`await` over raw promise chains.

## React

- Keep components focused: UI in components, data/effects in hooks or callers.
- Derive values during render when cheap; don’t mirror props into state.
- List keys must be stable IDs — not array indexes when order can change.
- Effects: declare full dependencies; don’t lie to the linter. Extract event handlers with `useCallback` only when needed for deps/memo children.
- Prefer composition (`children`, small wrappers) over deep prop drilling or giant config objects.
- Don’t add `useMemo`/`useCallback` by default — only for measured or required referential stability (context values, expensive work, memoized children).

## Accessibility (UI)

- Interactive elements need keyboard support and accessible names (`aria-label` / visible text).
- Don’t use `<div onClick>` when `<button>` / `<a>` is correct.
- Images that convey meaning need `alt`; decorative images use empty `alt`.
- Preserve focus management in modals/drawers (trap/restore as the existing pattern does).
